nown as aids were  reported in the united states  the outbreak grew into a pandemic that  has since claimed more than 34 million  lives  while treatments have come a long way  there is still  no vaccine and now some researchers in  the u.s say  kovan 19 has derailed the fight against  hiv aids  siphoning away health care workers and  other critical resources  jennifer johnson reports in san  francisco's golden gate park  a national aids memorial tribute  40 years after the u.s centers for  disease control reported the first cases  of a mysterious illness attacking  healthy gay men  aids was a terrifying diagnosis doctors  had no effective  treatment no matter how rich or famous  you were i was going to go back to my  home in california  and lock myself in my house and wait to  die because that's what we thought of  hiv  it was a death sentence but olympic gold  medal diver greg louganis doctor  his cousin told him to keep training and  compete in the 1988 games  i think it ultimately saved my life the  stigma of being hiv positive was  crushing to members of the gay community  the disease looked different to the  world after basketball star magic  johnson revealed his diagnosis in 1991.  researchers struggled for 15 years to  find effective life-prolonging  treatments  i think people tend to forget the sorrow  the difficulty the suffering that came  before that  we have made a remarkable progress  dr perry halquitus has been living with  hiv for more than 35 years  the virus has taken his former partner  and a close friend just a few days ago  i thought that part of my life was over  i thought the death of my friends was  over  and it's not but there is new hope  moderna which  race to create a covet 19 vaccine soon  plans to start clinical trials on a  potential hiv vaccine  currently anti-retroviral therapy helps  patients live longer healthy lives  but 1.5 million people are still being  diagnosed with hiv each year  and hundreds of thousands die this is a  disease that continues to exist  and one for which there is no vaccine  and one for which there is no cure  many believe the u.s could see its first  increase in aids infections in years  after so much medical attention and  research was focused on coven 19  why advocates continue to push  governments and the medical community to  find a cure  for decades after the virus was first  discovered  jennifer johnson global news washington  still ahead the unique tool in canada  shining new light  on the damage covered long-haulers face  you're watching global national  we're starting to learn more about the  long-term impact covid19 can have  on vital organs but there are now  troubling signs  about its effects on our vascular system  on this week's edition of the new  reality canadian science is shining a  light on the damage an  infection can leave behind here's donna  friesen with a preview donna  farah as people recuperate from covid19  researchers are looking into what lies  ahead for some of them who have  lingering symptoms  they're known as the long haulers and  one of the many things they're trying to  figure out is  what the virus does to blood vessels  this week on the new reality i talked to  one scientist at the university of  saskatchewan  he and his team are using the canadian  light source it's a light brighter than  the sun  to look incredibly deeply into blood  vessels  what we do see in the severe covid cases  is the lining on the on the inside of  the blood vessels  that becomes infected causes  inflammation  and swelling around the blood vessels  and what that causes is for for the  blood vessels to to contract and clamp  down  so there's there's just less area for  that  blood to flow through in the blood  vessels if you just  reduce blood flow to the heart you can  have  life-threatening arrhythmias so  disruptions in the contraction of the  heart  you can also have reduced kidney  function if there's reduced blood flow  to the kidneys  certainly the brain lots of people  report brain  fog and other cognitive impairments and  these  these are all part and parcel of  other vascular disorders that that have  already been well studied for decades  and we're now pulling a lot of these  pieces together and seeing that they  they do fit into this puzzle with covet  infection  the tool he is using to do this is  amazing it's the only one of its kind in  canada it's called  a synchrotron and it generates a beam of  light millions of times brighter than  the sun and that allows researchers to  look right down to the molecular level  at all kinds of material  including tissue from blood vessels the  point of this is to understand what the  long-term risk factors might be  for people who got coveted and survived  but there may be things going on inside  the blood vessels that could affect  major organs and could lead to higher  risks of things like stroke  and dementia even erectile dysfunction  it's fascinating research it's happening  right now here in canada  farah all right looking forward to the  story thank you donna donna friesen  and you can watch the full story on the  new reality tonight at 7 pm right here  on global  what do you get a legendary pilot for  their birthday  next the fly past fit for a major  milestone  on the eve of d-day a canadian fighter  pilot named stocky edwards turned  23 years old while serving overseas  the boy from saskatchewan had barely  seen a plane before signing up for the  military  he went on to become an aviation legend  so it's no surprise a fly pass was in  store  as his bc hometown celebrates its new  centenarian  the 100th birthday bash also featured a  drive past parade with over 200 cars  local dignitaries military members and  rcmp officers joined the public  celebration  beforehand edward sat down to reflect on  his life  in the skies i would still would like to  be a fighter pilot  somebody's had like riding a bicycle i  did 373  combat missions and the day it was  the war was over before this thing was  over  it was a feeling of what do i do now  i've been getting up every morning early  combat they were in the air so  all they wanted to do was go and do a  job for somebody  in the u of gifts people were asked to  donate to his wetland and wildlife  conservation fund or the stocky edwards  legacy trust a fund for young canadians  pursuing careers in aviation  his birthday wish has raised over 125  thousand dollars  so far and on behalf of all of us  happy birthday stocky and that's global  national for this saturday night i'm  farah nasser thank you for spending part  of your evening with us  tonight's your candidate is marsh lake  in yukon territory  thank you for watching i'll be back here  tomorrow have a great night
 

